<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#typhus">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Typhus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Typhus is a group of bacterial infectious diseases, often spread by lice, that cause fever and can be highly fatal in crowded or wartime conditions. It was among the Eurasian diseases introduced into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> after European contact. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">Smallpox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> and typhus killed an estimated 50–90 percent of the indigenous American population within a century of contact.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/typhus.jpg" alt="Typhus" width="300" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D. Raoult and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epidemic_typhus_Burundi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Typhus is a group of infectious diseases that includes epidemic typhus, scrub typhus and murine typhus. Common symptoms are fever, headache and a rash, typically beginning one to two weeks after exposure. Epidemic typhus is caused by the bacterium Rickettsia prowazekii and spread by body lice, scrub typhus by Orientia tsutsugamushi and spread by chiggers, and murine typhus by Rickettsia typhi and spread by fleas.</p><p>Epidemic typhus, the most severe form, generally occurs in outbreaks where poor sanitation and crowding are present. Without treatment, death may occur in 10 to 60 percent of cases. The antibiotic doxycycline is the treatment. The name comes from the Greek tûphos, meaning hazy or smoky, a word for delusion, and typhus is a different disease from typhoid fever.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Typhus has been described since at least 1528. The first reliable description appears in 1489, during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a> in the War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, when the Spanish lost 3,000 men to enemy action and a further 17,000 to typhus. Gaol fever, common in English prisons where inmates were crowded into dark, filthy rooms, is believed to have been typhus. In 1750 it killed the Lord Mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and many court officials at the Old Bailey, next to Newgate Prison.</p><p>Epidemics occurred routinely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the 16th to the 19th centuries. Typhus may have killed more than 10 percent of the German population in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, more French soldiers died of typhus and dysentery than were killed by the Russians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>An epidemic in Ireland between 1816 and 1819 killed an estimated 100,000 people, and typhus with typhoid fever is said to be responsible for 400,000 deaths in the Great Irish Famine of 1846 to 1849. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> typhus ravaged the armies of the Eastern Front, with over 150,000 deaths in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> alone. In 1922 the epidemic in Soviet territory peaked at some 20 to 30 million cases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and during the civil war it killed 2–3 million people.</p><p>Rudolf Weigl developed the first typhus vaccine in the interwar period; it did not prevent the disease but reduced mortality. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> typhus killed prisoners in camps and ghettos, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-frank">Anne Frank</a> and her sister Margot at Bergen-Belsen. Epidemic typhus is now rare.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Typhus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eurasian diseases including smallpox, measles and <b>typhus</b> killed an estimated 50 to 90 per cent of the pre-contact indigenous population within a century of contact, one of the most catastrophic demographic events in human history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Many historians blame epidemics of smallpox, influenza, measles and <b>typhus</b> above all; others, such as Andrés Reséndez, argue that enslavement and forced labour in the mines were a major killer, and David Stannard calls the encomienda a genocidal system.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
